>COROLLARY: If the above assumption is true, then a great new method of
>killing shopkeeper has just been invented. Anytime you find a shop with
>another shop on the level below, (as in Peter's game), dig a trapdoor
>directly in front of the shop doorway. Then dig your way in, collect
>the stuff and walk out. The shopkeeper will come running out the front
>door in pursuit, and fall staight down the trap door and into oblivion.

But if you can dig, why not just use the two-hole method and kick the
booty out of the shop?  This works for everything except potions, which
you can buy later after selling him back his own stolen merchandise.

For those who don't know about the two-hole method -- well, I won't go
through it.  It's well documented in the spoilers.  But it's easily the
best way to rob a shop.  Dwarves are fairly common creatures and almost
always carry a pickaxe.

For those confused by this: The two-hole method involves digging out two
areas of a shop and standing in one of them.  You then kick all of the
stuff out of a shop through the second hole.  So you're kicking items
out of a shop while not technically inside the shop.  I hope this is
removed in the next version because it really makes the game too easy.

And if the dual shopkeeper dilemma is in effect, where is the aborted
shopkeeper's money and key?  Do they vanish?  You would think they still
existed somewhere on the next lowest level.  
